WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.307
GROUPING SETS gives you explicit control over which summary levels are returned.

00:00:04.307 --> 00:00:08.512
Instead of generating a fixed hierarchy or every possible combination,

00:00:08.512 --> 00:00:10.867
you list the groupings you actually need.

00:00:10.867 --> 00:00:13.677
Run this example and look at the report levels.

00:00:13.677 --> 00:00:15.982
Compare them with ROLLUP and CUBE.

00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:22.177
This feature is useful when a report needs only selected totals, such as category totals and a grand total,

00:00:22.177 --> 00:00:24.242
but not every product subtotal.

00:00:24.242 --> 00:00:29.892
The output still contains rows at different grains, so label or interpret those rows carefully.

00:00:29.892 --> 00:00:34.502
Think of GROUPING SETS as a way to ask PostgreSQL for several GROUP BY queries at once,

00:00:34.502 --> 00:00:37.817
while keeping the requested summary levels under your control.

00:00:37.817 --> 00:00:41.747
Advanced grouping produces several aggregation levels in one result.

00:00:41.747 --> 00:00:47.687
The challenge is interpretation: NULL may mean a subtotal placeholder rather than missing source data.

00:00:47.687 --> 00:00:52.947
Use GROUPING metadata or explicit labels so downstream users can distinguish detail groups, subtotals,

00:00:52.947 --> 00:00:54.720
and grand totals.
